## TL;DR  
Yes – the `font-faces` block you use in a MapLibre Native stylesheet can be reused in a MapLibre GL JS style **as long as**:

| Requirement | What you need to do |
|-------------|----------------------|
| **GL JS version** | ≥ 6.7.0 (the version that reads `font-faces`). |
| **Font files reachable by the browser** | Serve the `.ttf/.otf` files over HTTP (or embed them as data-URIs). They can be local files bundled with the web app, but the browser must be able to fetch them. |
| **Offline use** | Either (a) omit the `glyphs` URL completely (GL JS ≥ 5.11 will fall back to local-font rendering for every script) **or** (b) keep a dummy `glyphs` URL and rely on `font-faces` for the scripts you care about. |
| **Runtime registration (optional)** | Call `map.setFontFaces()` after the map is created if you want to change the block without re-loading the style. |

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the gotchas you’ll hit when you try to make a truly offline web map.

---

## 1. Why `font-faces` works in GL JS

* **Since GL JS 6.7.0** the style spec’s root `font-faces` property is parsed and the listed font files are loaded through the browser’s normal `@font-face` mechanism.  
* The `text-font` names you use in your `symbol` layers are matched against the keys in `font-faces`. When a match is found, the browser-provided font is used **instead of** (or in addition to) the glyph-PBFs that would normally be fetched from the `glyphs` endpoint.  
* For complex scripts that need shaping (Devanagari, Thai, Khmer, Arabic, Hebrew, etc.) the browser’s text engine (or HarfBuzz in Native) does the heavy lifting, so the glyph-PBFs are ignored – the `font-faces` entry is the only thing that matters.

Because of this, the same JSON you wrote for Native can be dropped into a GL JS style **without any code changes** – the only difference is how the files are delivered.

---

## 2. Minimum GL JS version

| Feature | GL JS version |
|---------|----------------|
| Reads `font-faces` from the style at load time | **6.7.0** |
| `map.setFontFaces()` API (runtime update) | **6.7.0** |
| Local-font fallback when `glyphs` is missing or a range fails to load | **5.11.0** |
| Full shaping of Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, etc. (no RTL plugin) | **6.9.0** (Arabic/Hebrew) & **6.15.0** (grapheme-cluster shaping) |

If you are on an older GL JS build you must upgrade; otherwise the `font-faces` block will be ignored and the map will try to fetch glyph PBFs (and will fail offline).

---

## 3. Copying the block – what it looks like

### Native `font-faces` (example)

```json
{
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### GL JS-compatible version

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/does-not-matter/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",   // can be dummy or omitted
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "./fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari": [
      {
        "url": "./fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans Devanagari", "Noto Sans Regular"],
        "text-size": 12
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Key points**

* The `url` values must be **HTTP-accessible** from the page that runs GL JS.  
  * If you bundle the fonts with the web app, a relative URL like `./fonts/…` works.  
  * If you want a completely offline page (no network at all), you can embed the font as a **data-URI** (`"url": "data:font/ttf;base64,AAE…"`). This inflates the style JSON size but removes the network request.
* The `unicode-range` syntax is identical to the Native spec; you can keep it unchanged.
* The `glyphs` field can be omitted entirely. When it is missing GL JS ≥ 5.11 will **never** try to fetch a glyph PBF and will render everything via the local-font path (TinySDF for ideographs, browser shaping for complex scripts).  
  * If you keep a dummy URL you must make sure it returns a 404 quickly (or a tiny static file) – otherwise the browser will waste time trying to download it before falling back.

---

## 4. Making the web app truly offline

### 4.1. Serve the fonts from the same origin

* Put the `.ttf/.otf` files in a folder that is part of your web bundle (`public/fonts/…` for a Vite/React app, `static/fonts/…` for a plain HTML page, etc.).
* Ensure the server sends **CORS headers** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain) if you ever host the fonts on a different origin. Browsers block font loading without proper CORS.

### 4.2. Cache the fonts (optional but recommended)

* **Service Worker**: Cache the font files on the first load so subsequent visits work without a network connection.  
  ```js
  self.addEventListener('install', e => {
    e.waitUntil(
      caches.open('maplibre-fonts').then(cache => {
        return cache.addAll([
          '/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf',
          '/fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf'
        ]);
      })
    );
  });
  ```
* **Cache-Control** headers on the server (`Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable`) also help browsers keep the fonts in the HTTP cache.

### 4.3. Data-URI fallback (if you cannot serve files)

If you truly cannot make any HTTP request (e.g., a static HTML file opened from `file://`), embed the font:

```json
{
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "data:font/ttf;base64,AAEAAAARAQAABAA... (very long)",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

* The style JSON becomes large, but the browser never needs to fetch anything else.
* Keep the data-URI **under 2 MiB** per font; larger blobs may cause memory-pressure warnings on low-end devices.

---

## 5. Runtime registration (if you need to change fonts after the map is created)

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'style.json',          // contains the font-faces block
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  // If you want to add more fonts later:
  const extraFaces = {
    "My Brand Font": [
      {
        url: './fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf',
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]
      }
    ]
  };
  map.setFontFaces(extraFaces);
});
```

`setFontFaces` merges with the existing block, so you can add or replace fonts without re-loading the whole style.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Labels disappear on the first load, then appear after a second refresh** | Browser is trying to fetch a `glyphs` PBF that 404s, then falls back to local fonts. | Omit the `glyphs` field (or set it to a dummy URL that returns 404 instantly). |
| **CJK characters look wrong or are missing** | `localIdeographFontFamily` is set to `false` or you are serving CJK glyph PBFs that are huge and get blocked. | Keep the default (`'sans-serif'`) or set `localIdeographFontFamily` to a nice on-device CJK font. Do **not** serve CJK glyph PBFs unless you really need the exact shapes. |
| **Devanagari text shows as separate letters, not combined** | Using a GL JS version < 6.15 or a `glyphs` URL that forces per-codepoint rendering. | Upgrade to GL JS ≥ 6.15 (or at least 6.7) and keep the `font-faces` entry for the Devanagari font. |
| **Arabic text is rendered left-to-right** | GL JS version < 6.9 and no RTL plugin loaded. | Upgrade to ≥ 6.9 (no plugin needed) **or** load the RTL plugin as a temporary workaround. |
| **Fonts load in Chrome but not in Firefox** | Missing CORS header on the font file. | Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) to the HTTP response for the font files. |
| **Offline page works on desktop but not on mobile** | Mobile browsers block `file://` fonts or have stricter CORS. | Serve the page over `http://localhost` (e.g., via a simple `python -m http.server`) or embed fonts as data-URIs. |

---

