Below is a step-by-step recipe for adding a **hillshade** layer that draws its data from a **raster-DEM** source to a MapLibre GL JS map (or to a style JSON you’re editing).  
Everything you need is in the code snippets – just copy-paste, adjust the URLs/IDs, and you’re good to go.

---

## 1. Make sure you have a raster-DEM source

A hillshade layer can only read from a source whose `type` is `"raster-dem"`.  
If you already have a DEM tile service (e.g. a Mapbox-style `tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` or a Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF endpoint that serves raster tiles), add it to the style:

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (you can also put this in your style JSON)
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // URL template for the DEM tiles (must return 256-pixel PNG/JPEG tiles)
  // Example: OpenTopoMap, USGS, or your own tileset
  tiles: [
    'https://example.com/tiles/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'   // <-- replace
  ],
  // Optional: the pixel size of each DEM sample (meters per pixel)
  // If you don’t know it, 30 is a safe default for most 30 m DEMs.
  tileSize: 256,
  // If your DEM tiles are in a different CRS (e.g. EPSG:3857) you can
  // leave this out – MapLibre assumes Web Mercator.
  // If you need to set a different maxzoom/minzoom, do it here.
  maxzoom: 14,
  attribution: '© Your DEM provider'
});
```

> **Tip:** If you already have a *terrain* source (used for 3-D extrusion), you can reuse it – the same source works for both `terrain` and `hillshade`.

---

## 2. Add the hillshade layer

A hillshade layer is a **type `"hillshade"`**. It reads the elevation values from the raster-DEM source and renders a shaded relief image.

```js
// 2️⃣ Add the hillshade layer (drawn on top of the base map, under labels)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',          // matches the source ID you added above
  // If your DEM source has multiple sub-layers (rare for raster-dem),
  // you could specify `source-layer`, but most DEM tiles have only one.
  paint: {
    // ---- Basic visual controls ------------------------------------------------
    // Exaggerate the relief (default 0.5). Larger values make mountains look steeper.
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,

    // Light direction (azimuth) in degrees clockwise from north (0-360)
    // 315° = north-west light (the classic “shaded relief” look)
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,

    // Light intensity (0-1). 1 = full contrast, 0 = flat gray.
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'viewport', // or 'map' for map-relative lighting
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',   // optional, default is black 0.5
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)', // optional
    // You can also tweak the contrast with:
    // 'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.2)'
  }
}, 'waterway-label'); // <-- optional: insert before a label layer so labels stay on top
```

### What the paint properties do

| Property | Typical range | What it changes |
|----------|---------------|-----------------|
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | 0 – 2 (default 0.5) | Height scaling – higher = steeper look |
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | 0 – 360 (default 315) | Sun azimuth – rotate to change shadow direction |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `"viewport"` or `"map"` (default `viewport`) | Whether the light stays fixed on the screen or rotates with the map |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | any CSS color (default `rgba(0,0,0,0.5)`) | Darkness of shadows |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | any CSS color (default `rgba(255,255,255,0.5)`) | Brightness of illuminated slopes |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | any CSS color (default `rgba(255,255,255,0)`) | Optional extra highlight for very steep faces |

You can experiment with these values until the relief matches the visual style you want.

---

## 3. (Optional) Add a terrain source for 3-D extrusion

If you also want 3-D terrain (e.g. extruded buildings, fly-over), you need to enable the **terrain** property on the map:

```js
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'my-dem',          // same DEM source as the hillshade
  exaggeration: 1.5          // optional – makes mountains taller in 3-D
});
```

The hillshade will continue to work independently; the terrain setting only affects the globe/3-D view.

---

## 4. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from Raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map (e.g. OSM raster tiles)
        'osm': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        },
        // 1️⃣ DEM source for hillshade (and optional terrain)
        'my-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          tiles: ['https://example.com/tiles/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 14,
          attribution: '© DEM provider'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // Base raster map
        { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' },

        // 2️⃣ Hillshade layer (drawn after the base map)
        {
          id: 'hillshade',
          type: 'hillshade',
          source: 'my-dem',
          paint: {
            'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,
            'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
            'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
            'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    // Optional: set a nicer on-device CJK font if you have one
    // localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif'
  });

  // Optional: enable 3-D terrain (does not affect hillshade)
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace* `https://example.com/tiles/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` with the URL template of your own DEM tiles. The rest works out-of-the-box.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Hillshade is missing or appears as a solid gray** | The DEM source isn’t loading (404, CORS, wrong tile size) | Open the tile URL in a browser; ensure the server returns a 256 px PNG/JPEG and sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain). |
| **Hillshade looks “flat” (no shadows)** | `hillshade-exaggeration` is too low (default 0.5) or the DEM resolution is coarse. | Increase `hillshade-exaggeration` (e.g., 1.0-1.5) or use a higher-resolution DEM. |
| **Shadows point the wrong way** | `hillshade-illumination-direction` is set to an unexpected angle. | Adjust the angle (0 = north, 90 = east, 180 = south, 270 = west). |
| **Labels disappear under the hillshade** | The hillshade layer is added *after* label layers, covering them. | Insert the hillshade layer **before** label layers (use the optional second argument to `addLayer` to specify a layer ID that should stay on top). |
| **CORS error in console** | Your DEM tiles are on a different origin without proper CORS headers. | Configure the tile server to send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or the specific origin). If you can’t change the server, proxy the tiles through a CORS-enabled endpoint. |
| **Hillshade looks pixelated at high zoom** | The DEM tiles are low-resolution (e.g., maxzoom = 10). | Serve higher-zoom DEM tiles or increase `maxzoom` in the source definition. |

---

## 6. Quick checklist before you ship

1. **Source** – `type: "raster-dem"` with a working tile URL and correct `tileSize` (256 is standard).  
2. **Layer** – `type: "hillshade"` referencing that source.  
3. **Paint** – tweak `hillshade-exaggeration` and `hillshade-illumination-direction` to get the desired visual feel.  
4. **Layer order** – place the hillshade **below** any label or symbol layers you want to stay on top.  
5. **CORS** – ensure the DEM tiles are accessible from the browser.  
6. **Optional terrain** – call `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem'})` if you need 3-D terrain as well.

That’s it! Once the source and layer are in place, MapLibre GL JS will automatically generate the shaded relief on the fly, giving your map a nice topographic feel without any extra processing on your side. Happy mapping!
